203:\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:approxdistanceishard}}
209:We prove Theorem~\ref{thm:approxdistanceishard} by reducing from the problem of approximating the longest directed cycle in a digraph. We rely on the following two results from Bj\"{o}rklund, Husfeldt, and Khanna given as Theorems 1 and 2 in~\cite{BHK04}. 
212:  Consider the problem of computing a long directed cycle in a given Hamiltonian digraph $D$ on $n$ vertices.
214:  \item For every fixed $\varepsilon>0$, unless $\P=\NP$, there does not exist any polynomial-time algorithm that returns a directed cycle of length at least $n^{\varepsilon}$ in $D$.
215:  \item For every polynomial-time computable increasing function $f:\mathbb{N}\to \mathbb{N}$ in $\omega(1)$, unless the Exponential Time Hypothesis fails, there does not exist any
216:    polynomial-time algorithm that returns a directed cycle of length at least $f(n) \log n$ in $D$.
220:Note that in the two problems, the input graph is guaranteed to be Hamiltonian, yet it remains hard to explicitly \emph{construct} a directed cycle of some guaranteed length. Characterising the approximability of the longest cycle problem in undirected graphs is a longstanding open question~\cite{AYZ95,GN08}. 
222:%Since in order to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:approxdistanceishard} we will have to construct bipartite graphs of maximum degree $3$, the following slightly modified variants of the above two results will be more convenient for us. With a slight abuse of notation, in the following we will refer to a digraph $D$ as \emph{subcubic} if it is an orientation of a graph of maximum degree at most $3$ and no vertex of $D$ is a sink or a source. 
225:%Let $\varepsilon>0$ be fixed. Unless \P$=$\NP, there exists no polynomial-time algorithm that given as input an $n$-vertex \emph{subcubic} digraph $D$ with a directed cycle of length at least $\Omega(\sqrt{n})$ computes a directed cycle of length at least $n^{\varepsilon}$ in $D$. 
229:%Let $f:\mathbb{N}\rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ be a polynomial-time computable increasing function in $\omega(1)$. 
230:%Then, unless the Exponential Time Hypothesis fails, there exists no polynomial-time algorithm that, given as input an $n$-vertex \emph{subcubic} digraph $D$ containing a directed cycle of length at least $\Omega(\sqrt{n})$, computes a directed cycle of length at least $f(n) \log^2 n$ in $D$. 
235:%For every vertex $v$ of $D$ we create (in polynomial time) two balanced binary trees $T_v^+$ and $T_v^-$, where both of these trees have a root vertex of degree $1$ and $T_v^+$ has exactly $\text{deg}_D^+(v)$ many leafs while $T_v^-$ has exactly $\text{deg}_D^-(v)$ many leafs. Note that since we created binary trees and $\text{deg}_D^+(v), \text{deg}_D^-(v) < n$, $T_v^+$ and $T_v^-$ both have depth $O(\log n)$. Furthermore, we orient the edges of the trees such that $T_v^+$ is an out-tree, $T_v^-$ is an in-tree, and we identify their root vertices with $v$. Finally, for every arc $(u,v)$ in $D$ we add an arc from a leaf of $T_u^+$ to one of the leafs of $T_v^-$ such that different arcs start and end at different leafs. 
237:%The resulting digraph $D'$ is easily seen to be subcubic, it has $O(n^2)$ vertices and furthermore the construction directly yields a one-to-one correspondence between directed cycles $C$ in $D$ with directed cycles $C'$ in $D'$ such that $\ell(C) \le \ell(C') \le O(\log n)\ell(C)$ holds. Using this relationships between the cycle lengths in $D$ and $D'$ and since $D'$ can be constructed from $D$ in polynomial-time, the above corollaries directly follow from the previously stated hardness results by Bj\"{o}rklund et al.
245:There exists a polynomial-time algorithm that, given as input a number $\ell$, the orientations $D_0,\ldots,D_t$ and a directed cycle $C$ in $D_t$ of length $|C|>\ell^{t+1}$, computes a directed cycle in $D_0$ of length at least $\ell$. 
248:The bound of Lemma~\ref{lem:cycle} can be shown to be essentially tight. We refer to Figure~\ref{fig:cycle} for an illustration of the construction.
