Lemma 5 ([7]) Consider the subgraph in Fig. 2. Suppose that are two vertex
disjoint paths passing through the subgraph—one leaving at A and the other
entering at B. Then the path leaving A must have entered at C and the path
entering at B must leave at D. Furthermore, there is exactly one additional path
through the subgraph and it connects either E to F or G to H, depending on the
actual routing of the path leaving at A.
    Also, if one of these additional paths is present, all vertices are traversed.

     To prove Prop. 1 we reduce from Monotone 1-in-3-Satisfiability, rather than
3-Satisfiability as used in [7]. An instance of 1-in-3-Sat is a Boolean expression
in conjunctive normal form in which every clause has three literals. The question
is if there is a truth assignment such that in every clause, exactly one literal is
true. It is known that even when all literals are positive (Monotone 1-in-3-Sat)
the problem is NP-complete [15].
     Given such an instance ϕ with clauses t1 , . . . , tm on variables x1 , . . . , xn we
construct and instance Gϕ of R2VDP as follows.


